# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever checks the repo out.
#
# Git for Windows defaults core.autocrlf to true, which rewrites LF to CRLF
# in the working tree. For most repositories that is harmless. Here it breaks
# two things outright:
#
#   - the golden files (unify/test/golden/, lens/test/golden/) are compared
#     byte for byte against a program's stdout, which is LF. A CRLF checkout
#     fails every golden test on Windows and the diff shows two lines that
#     look identical.
#   - the test harnesses are bash scripts; CRLF in a shell script produces
#     "\r: command not found" or worse, silently.
#
# So: LF in the working tree everywhere, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf
